"indexedAt": "2026-11-03T03:40:42.173Z", "labels": []}, {"uri": "at://did:plc:vxzqeu2zbp4bviwvmqbixlps/app.bsky.feed.post/3mzew4xt7v62l", "cid": "bafyreifu67utn6kb5ge7af7dwy52ixwhpzo5qxughgzhyj6b2jzz72bvz4", "author": {"did": "did:plc:vxzqeu2zbp4bviwvmqbixlps", "handle": "killbaitcanada.bsky.social", "displayName": "KillBait Canada \ud83c\udde8\ud83c\udde6", "avatar": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/avatar/plain/did:plc:vxzqeu2zbp4bviwvmqbixlps/bafkreifchcvluaedpm6g6rgmcue56c6isgkdkuyoheq3dyokymo2eyegbq", "associated": {"activitySubscription": {"allowSubscriptions": "followers"}}, "labels": [{"src": "did:plc:vxzqeu2zbp4bviwvmqbixlps", "uri": "at://did:plc:vxzqeu2zbp4bviwvmqbixlps/app.bsky.actor.profile/self", "cid": "bafyreiafnjb7brqvdcqkc5gqzq35f4q6wrzeu3gp7jiutflmmo2vvkzg3m", "val": "bot", "cts": "2026-05-11T06:29:22.256Z"}], "createdAt": "2026-05-11T06:29:23.662Z"}, "record": {"$type": "app.bsky.feed.post", "createdAt": "2026-11-03T03:40:24.956794500Z", "embed": {"$type": "app.bsky.embed.external", "external": {"description": "The Oklahoma City Thunder will retire Russell Westbrook's No. 0 jersey on February 7, honouring the former NBA Most Valuable Player for his contributions to the franchise. The ceremony will take place when the Thunder host the New York Knicks. Westbrook, 37, spent 11 of his 18 NBA seasons with Oklahoma City, establishing himself as one of the most accomplished players in the team's history. He finished his Thunder career as the franchise's all-time leader in games played (821), points (18,859), rebounds (5,760), assists (6,897), steals (1,442), field goals made (6,626) and minutes played (28,329). During his time with the franchise, Oklahoma City recorded 538 wins and 348 losses, achieving the NBA's third-best winning percentage during that period. The Thunder reached the NBA Finals in 2012, qualified for the playoffs nine times and made four appearances in the Western Conference Finals. Westbrook's most outstanding individual achievement came during the 2016-17 season, when he won the NBA MVP award. He averaged 31.6 points, 10.7 rebounds, 10.4 assists and 1.63 steals per game, becoming only the second player in league history to average a triple-double over an entire season. His 42 triple-doubles that campaign remain an NBA single-season record. He also holds the NBA career record for triple-doubles, with 209, further cementing his place among the league's most statistically accomplished players. Thunder general manager Sam Presti praised Westbrook's contributions both on and off the court, highlighting his determination, commitment and involvement in the community. Presti said Westbrook's legacy had made him an important figure in Oklahoma, where he is widely regarded as one of the region's own. The retirement of his jersey number will permanently recognize his achievements and lasting connection with the Oklahoma City franchise.", "title": "Oklahoma City Thunder to retire Russell Westbrook's No. 0 jersey in February", "uri": "https://en.killbait.com/oklahoma-city-thunder-to-retire-russell-westbrook-s-no-0-jersey-in-february.html?utm_source=bluesky&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=killbaitcanada.bsky.social"}}, "facets": [{"features": [{"$type": "app.bsky.richtext.facet#tag", "tag": "baloncesto"}], "index": {"byteEnd": 157, "byteStart": 146}}, {"features": [{"$type": "app.bsky.richtext.facet#tag", "tag": "nba"}], "index": {"byteEnd": 162, "byteStart": 158}}, {"features": [{"$type": "app.bsky.richtext.facet#tag", "tag": "russellwestbrook"}], "index": {"byteEnd": 180, "byteStart": 163}}], "text": "Oklahoma City Thunder to retire Russell Westbrook's No. 0 jersey in February\n\n\ud83e\udd16 IA: It's not clickbait \u2705\n\ud83d\udc65 Users: It's not clickbait \u2705\n\n#baloncesto #nba #russellwestbrook \n\n\ud83d\udc47\ud83d\udc47\ud83d\udc47"}, "embed": {"external": {"uri": "https://en.killbait.com/oklahoma-city-thunder-to-retire-russell-westbrook-s-no-0-jersey-in-february.html?utm_source=bluesky&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=killbaitcanada.bsky.social", "title": "Oklahoma City Thunder to retire Russell Westbrook's No. 0 jersey in February", "description": "The Oklahoma City Thunder will retire Russell Westbrook's No. 0 jersey on February 7, honouring the former NBA Most Valuable Player for his contributions to the franchise. The ceremony will take place when the Thunder host the New York Knicks. Westbrook, 37, spent 11 of his 18 NBA seasons with Oklahoma City, establishing himself as one of the most accomplished players in the team's history. He finished his Thunder career as the franchise's all-time leader in games played (821), points (18,859), rebounds (5,760), assists (6,897), steals (1,442), field goals made (6,626) and minutes played (28,329). During his time with the franchise, Oklahoma City recorded 538 wins and 348 losses, achieving the NBA's third-best winning percentage during that period. The Thunder reached the NBA Finals in 2012, qualified for the playoffs nine times and made four appearances in the Western Conference Finals. Westbrook's most outstanding individual achievement came during the 2016-17 season, when he won the NBA MVP award. He averaged 31.6 points, 10.7 rebounds, 10.4 assists and 1.63 steals per game, becoming only the second player in league history to average a triple-double over an entire season. His 42 triple-doubles that campaign remain an NBA single-season record. He also holds the NBA career record for triple-doubles, with 209, further cementing his place among the league's most statistically accomplished players. Thunder general manager Sam Presti praised Westbrook's contributions both on and off the court, highlighting his determination, commitment and involvement in the community. Presti said Westbrook's legacy had made him an important figure in Oklahoma, where he is widely regarded as one of the region's own. The retirement of his jersey number will permanently recognize his achievements and lasting connection with the Oklahoma City franchise."}, "$type": "app.bsky.embed.external#view"}, "bookmarkCount": 0, "replyCount": 0, "repostCount": 0, "likeCount": 0, "quoteCount": 0, "indexedAt": "2026-11-03T03:40:25.775Z", "labels": []}, {"uri": "at://did:plc:6j5hgfdiysqh5wn5lsjer3bb/app.bsky.feed.post/3mzevyzds5k2y", "cid": "bafyreic6efikarzmrte6cvfgy3ou4cpabeunquhbjowp6rysy7iehhv3ei", "author": {"did": "did:plc:6j5hgfdiysqh5wn5lsjer3bb", "handle": "stephkarekare.bsky.social", "displayName": "Steph Kare-Kare", "avatar": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/avatar/plain/did:plc:6j5hgfdiysqh5wn5lsjer3bb/bafkreif5qxckuq2tloy67mlqv2rorliumwc7mppz2oedtl6asof4tl222i", "associated": {"activitySubscription": {"allowSubscriptions": "followers"}}, "labels": [], "createdAt": "2024-11-13T18:03:45.178Z"}, "record": {"$type": "app.bsky.feed.post", "createdAt": "2026-11-03T03:38:12.773Z", "langs": ["en"], "text": "The Thunder have mentioned they wish they celebrated more the summer they won. So I\u2019m happy the Knicks were outside all summer. \n\nBut they look sluggish, and are definitely paying that price now.\n\nThey\u2019ll get it together, but it\u2019s tough right now."}, "bookmarkCount": 0, "replyCount": 0, "repostCount": 0, "likeCount": 0, "quoteCount": 0, "indexedAt": "2026-11-03T03:38:12.974Z", "labels": []}, {"uri": "at://did:plc:yaofg6zwpsdp26f5cnf6wnx5/app.bsky.feed.post/3mzevyv3w672n", "cid": "bafyreic4jq7va72hivzsicp2qhlqdyqpbw7bc3gnqmi32gcof53vwf7yte", "author": {"did": "did:plc:yaofg6zwpsdp26f5cnf6wnx5", "handle": "nypostsports.bsky.social", "displayName": "New York Post Sports", "avatar": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/avatar/plain/did:plc:yaofg6zwpsdp26f5cnf6wnx5/bafkreiftffaz4zp2l4u6bti3jkr33fjvl6fxrbhhkenbaed6d52dc4bdvq", "associated": {"activitySubscription": {"allowSubscriptions": "followers"}}, "labels": [], "createdAt": "2024-11-25T14:14:32.046Z"}, "record": {"$type": "app.bsky.feed.post", "createdAt": "2026-11-03T03:38:07.995427Z", "embed": {"$type": "app.bsky.embed.external", "external": {"descripti